<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uzbek Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1282">1282</a>–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Öz Beg Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> Khan was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1313 to 1341, the longest reign in its history. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the state religion, built mosques in the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He controlled the Russian principalities by granting the title of grand prince, favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> over Tver, and the Horde's trade and power reached their height in his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan.jpg" alt="Uzbek Khan" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Dalorto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Khan_Uzbek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Öz Beg, also known as Giyas ad-Din Muhammad Uzbek Khan, was born in 1282, the son of Toghrilcha and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengu-timur">Mengu-Timur</a>, who had been khan from 1266 to 1280. His father was one of the Genghisid princes who overthrew Tode-Mengu and was later executed by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhta">Toqta</a>. Toqta took Toghrilcha's widow as his wife and sent Öz Beg into exile in a distant region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, either Khorazm or the country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>.</p><p>Öz Beg was converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by Ibn Abdul Hamid, a Sunni Sufi Bukharan sayyid and sheikh of the Yasavi order, and took the throne on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhta">Toqta</a>'s death in January 1313 with the help of the vizier Temur Qutlugh and Bulaghan khatun. Many Mongol nobles feared being forced to adopt Islamic law and plotted to kill him, but he crushed the rebels. His last rebellious relative, the shamanist Ilbasan, was murdered in 1320.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Öz Beg kept one of the largest armies in the world, said to exceed 300,000 warriors, and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in 1319, 1325 and 1335. He found an ally in Mamluk Egypt, but the alliance ended after Egypt made peace with the Ilkhanate in 1323. His armies raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 1324 and 1337 and took 300,000 captives. In 1332 he let Venetians establish a colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Tanais</a> on the Don. Sarai grew into a main commercial centre, and he built a new city, Saray al-Jedid, or New Sarai.</p><p>He confirmed the tax exemption of Russian clergy, and a letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> thanked him for his treatment of Christians. He supported the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> against their relatives of Tver. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-of-moscow">Yury of Moscow</a> grand prince, and four princes of Tver were killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> at his behest, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail of Tver</a>, beheaded in November 1318. In 1331 he appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I of Moscow</a> grand prince of Vladimir, which marked the true beginning of the rise of the Muscovites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Öz Beg died in 1341, reportedly in October or November, and was succeeded by his son Tini Beg. He was the longest-reigning khan, and the state reached its zenith under him. Arab and Persian authors of the 14th and 15th centuries described him as an able statesman. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who had an audience with him in 1333, wrote that he was one of the seven most powerful kings of the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Özbeg Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Berke (r. 1257–1266) was the first khan to become Muslim, and under <b>Uzbek Khan</b> (r. 1313–1341) Islam became the religion of the state, though the khans protected the Orthodox Church and exempted its clergy from tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Yury married a sister of <b>Uzbek Khan</b> of the Golden Horde and obtained the khan's patent (yarlyk) for the grand principality of Vladimir in 1317, beginning a bitter rivalry with the princes of Tver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its khans, including Berke, <b>Uzbek Khan</b> and Jani Beg, held authority over the whole Jochid realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/blue-horde">Blue Horde</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Golden Horde. Died in Sarai. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
